I want to talk about a really  interesting email that came in from a  viewer and it's so interesting in fact  that I want to devote a segment to it  today this is an email i received from  alan who wrote to me and said david do  you think straw Manning  oversimplification and extremism  is necessary in order to initiate  movement regarding an issue a lot of  people don't have the interest patience  time or intelligence to really dig deep  on an issue of course straw Manning can  be very unfair and obviously very  divisive but to some degree I think that  it can be an effective way to fight the  battle of ideas for example the  right-wing stance on guns is often seen  by the left as a result of rich  politicians and gun owners not caring  about the slaughter of children I  personally don't buy that but if I did  I'd be pissed off and yelling in the  streets too so basically what Alan is  asking me is whether the simple  unenhanced unembellished truth is enough  to inspire activism or whether you sort  of need to exaggerate the other side's  position to get people activating around  a political issue so let's take the  three one at a time right we have straw  Manning oversimplification and extremism  straw Manning straw Manning is  distorting someone else's position to  make it easier to refute or argue  against so you could strawman the  pro-gun views of people by saying they  don't care about dead children is that  literally true but probably not but if  you believe that their policy in support  of gun rights leads to dead children you  might tell yourself I'm okay straw  Manning their position because it's a  useful idea to get support another  example would be like imagine a  politician who's in favor of tax cuts  for the rich you could strawman their  position by saying they don't care about  destroying the economy when the truth is  they just don't believe tax cuts for the  rich are bad for the economy and it's  much easier to argue against the straw  man position I don't think straw Manning  is necessary to create political  activism I also don't think extremism is  necessary to create political activism  but I do want to talk about  simplification and oversimplification I  think that it is very useful maybe not  always necessary but oversimplification  often a good tool the truth is that life  is complex most people don't work in  politics most people they're busy living  their lives and earning a living and it  is often useful and not dishonest to  simplify an issue in order to get people  to see why it's important most issues  just aren't black and white but that  doesn't mean that it's a huge gray area  that's not closer to one side or the  other I believe it is not dishonest to  simplify issues for example abortion  being about controlling women the truth  is that it's more complex right there's  a debate about when life starts there's  a big religious component there's a lot  going on but the impact of the religious  rights position and the conservative  rights position on abortion is one where  women are not going to be allowed to  decide about their own bodies period so  I believe that it is useful to simplify  the issue in those terms  I believe it's useful in not dishonest  to simplify the issue of net neutrality  to freedom on the internet from  corporate power it's more complex its  regulatory its technical there's a lot  of stuff going on but in some  fundamental way that really is what it's  about it's about freedom from corporate  power on the Internet  climate change to give one more example  climate change is nuanced it requires  some understanding of science it can be  technical but it's not wrong to inspire  activism by saying we're wrecking the  only planet we have and every day we  don't fix it we're closer to our own  destruction that will catalyze activism  much more than talking about carbon data  from the last 800,000 years and I do  think that it's useful and not at all  dishonest some people may disagree with  me others may agree with the idea that  simplification is OK but not on the  issues that I think it's OK on but I'm  really really interested to hear from  people on this is it fundamentally  dishonest to simplify an issue not to  strawman not to take extremists  positions but to simplify and distill it  to its most important points I think the  answer is that it's ok but I want to  hear from people about this today's  episode of the show is made possible in  part by class  if you've ever wanted to sit in on  classes taught by world-class professors  at America's top universities without  actually having to attend those  universities in pay tuition class  central is the place to go it's a  comprehensive free search engine that  lets you find exactly the type of online  course you're looking for there are  courses from a ton of different  providers taught by some of the most  qualified professors in the country  thousands of courses to choose from many  of them totally free including courses  from Ivy League schools like Harvard  Yale Princeton other top schools like  Stanford and MIT you can search courses  by subject by University you can see  which courses are trending and you can  also rate and review courses and because  other users have already done this you  can see what other people have actually  said about the courses you're thinking  of taking I've been checking out a lot  of their courses on thinking both  critically and philosophically it's been  absolutely fascinating but they have  everything from political science to  philosophy economics math physics Comp  Sci business graphic design really  anything you can think of and you can  start taking classes taught by top tier  professors from the best universities in  the US right now by going to class  central.com slash Pakman that's class -  central slash PA que ma n I've also put  the link under this video if you're  watching on YouTube
 

